# Recipe: NDJSON Messaging Over Hyperswarm

> **Legacy standalone protocol only.** New app messaging uses
> `@unstoppable-stack/p2p/rpc` with `/service` or an existing root node.
> Do not send raw NDJSON into the library-owned multiplexed connection.
> This example does not supply pairing/grants or production message bounds.


## Problem

Send lightweight JSON messages between peers without Protomux or RPC framing.

## Prerequisites

- Hyperswarm installed
- Basic understanding of Node.js streams

## Solution

### Step 1: Define a newline-delimited JSON protocol

Each message is one JSON object per line. This keeps framing simple and avoids
partial JSON parse errors.

```ts
type WireMessage = {
  type: "chat" | "ping" | "pong";
  id?: string;
  payload?: Record<string, unknown>;
};
```

### Step 2: Parse incoming stream data with a buffer

```ts
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let buffer = "";

conn.on("data", (chunk: Buffer) => {
  buffer += decoder.decode(chunk, { stream: true });
  const lines = buffer.split("\n");
  buffer = lines.pop() ?? "";

  for (const line of lines) {
    if (!line.trim()) continue;
    const msg = JSON.parse(line) as WireMessage;
    handleMessage(msg);
  }
});
```

### Step 3: Serialize outgoing messages with a newline

```ts
function sendMessage(msg: WireMessage) {
  conn.write(`${JSON.stringify(msg)}\n`);
}
```

## Full Example

```ts
import Hyperswarm from "hyperswarm";

type WireMessage = {
  type: "chat" | "ping" | "pong";
  id?: string;
  payload?: Record<string, unknown>;
};

const swarm = new Hyperswarm();
const topic = Buffer.alloc(32).fill("ndjson-demo");

swarm.on("connection", (conn, info) => {
  const decoder = new TextDecoder();
  let buffer = "";

  function handleMessage(msg: WireMessage) {
    if (msg.type === "ping") {
      sendMessage({ type: "pong", id: msg.id });
      return;
    }

    if (msg.type === "chat") {
      console.log("chat:", msg.payload);
    }
  }

  function sendMessage(msg: WireMessage) {
    conn.write(`${JSON.stringify(msg)}\n`);
  }

  conn.on("data", (chunk: Buffer) => {
    buffer += decoder.decode(chunk, { stream: true });
    const lines = buffer.split("\n");
    buffer = lines.pop() ?? "";

    for (const line of lines) {
      if (!line.trim()) continue;
      handleMessage(JSON.parse(line) as WireMessage);
    }
  });

  conn.on("close", () => console.log("peer disconnected", info.publicKey));
  conn.on("error", (err) => console.error("conn error", err));

  if (info.client) {
    sendMessage({ type: "ping", id: crypto.randomUUID() });
    sendMessage({ type: "chat", payload: { text: "hello" } });
  }
});

const discovery = swarm.join(topic, { server: true, client: true });
await discovery.flushed();
```

## Variations

### Variation 1: Add message size limits

If you accept untrusted peers, cap line length before parsing to avoid memory
blowups.

```ts
const MAX_LINE = 64 * 1024;
if (line.length > MAX_LINE) throw new Error("message too large");
```

### Variation 2: Add acks and retries

Attach `id` fields and keep a map of pending messages. Retry if you do not
receive a `pong`/`ack` within a timeout.

## Troubleshooting

### Problem: JSON parse errors on incoming data

**Symptoms**: `SyntaxError: Unexpected end of JSON input`. **Solution**: Ensure
you buffer chunks and only parse full lines. Never parse partial stream chunks
directly.

### Problem: Messages arrive merged together

**Symptoms**: Two JSON objects appear concatenated without separators.
**Solution**: Always add `\n` after each `JSON.stringify` and split on newline
when reading.

